/**
\defgroup distrib_mcp Properized MCP
\brief Continuous real-line distribution whose negative log-density is Zhang's minimax concave penalty on an interval around the location and a Gaussian beyond it, with a normalizing constant in closed form through Dawson's integral.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/mcp.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::mcp_distrib "mcp_distrib", this module gives the log-density and its partial derivatives to order \f$6\f$ in the four parameters, the mixed derivatives in the observation and the parameters, the cumulative functions with their logarithms, the non-smooth points and starting values. Every member that evaluates the law is marked `LAPLACE_HD`, so it runs on the host and inside Kokkos kernels; `start_value()` runs on the host only. Against mpmath references (mpmath), the worst measured error of the log-density and its derivatives is \f$162\f$ ulp of the scale of their order, and that of the logarithms of the cumulative functions is \f$2.46\f$ ulp of \f$\max(\lvert \log F \rvert, 1)\f$; the measures are those of \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured", and the figures are in the section \"Accuracy and limits\" below.

## Classic form and parametrization

The minimax concave penalty of Zhang (Zhang, 2010) is a function of \f$u = |y - \mu|\f$, the distance of an observation \f$y \in \mathbb{R}\f$ from a location \f$\mu \in \mathbb{R}\f$. It has a level \f$\lambda > 0\f$ and a concavity \f$\gamma > 0\f$, and it is written in two pieces,
\f[
\rho(u) = \begin{cases} \lambda u - \dfrac{u^2}{2\gamma}, & u \le \gamma\lambda, \\[4pt] \dfrac{\gamma\lambda^2}{2}, & u > \gamma\lambda. \end{cases}
\f]
The knot \f$u = \gamma\lambda\f$ separates the two pieces. Beyond the knot \f$\rho\f$ is flat, so \f$e^{-\rho(u)}\f$ has an infinite integral for every value of the parameters. The law keeps \f$\rho\f$ on the inner piece and replaces the flat piece by a Gaussian tail with variance \f$V > 0\f$, the tail variance:
\f[
q(u) = \begin{cases} \lambda u - \dfrac{u^2}{2\gamma}, & u \le \gamma\lambda, \\[4pt] \dfrac{\gamma\lambda^2}{2} + \dfrac{(u - \gamma\lambda)^2}{2V}, & u > \gamma\lambda, \end{cases}
\f]
and the density is
\f[
f(y) = \frac{e^{-q(|y - \mu|)}}{Z}, \qquad y \in \mathbb{R},
\f]
with \f$Z\f$ the normalizing constant. The log-density is therefore \f$\log f(y) = -q(|y - \mu|) - \log Z\f$.

The parameters are readable from \f$q\f$. The location \f$\mu\f$ is the mode and the median of the law, and the density is symmetric about it, so its mean is \f$\mu\f$ as well. The rate \f$\lambda\f$ is the slope of \f$q\f$ at the location, because \f$q'(0^+) = \lambda\f$, and the concavity \f$\gamma\f$ places the knot at \f$\gamma\lambda\f$. On the inner piece the exponent of the density is the penalty \f$\rho\f$ of Zhang, which is what gives the parameters their meaning. The SCAD law of \ref distrib_scad "the Properized SCAD page" has the same property with the penalty of Fan and Li (Fan and Li, 2001).

## Density and normalizing constant

The normalizing constant is
\f[
Z = 2\left[\sqrt{2\gamma}\,\mathcal{D}\!\left(\lambda\sqrt{\gamma/2}\right) + e^{-\gamma\lambda^2/2}\sqrt{\pi V/2}\right],
\f]
where \f$\mathcal{D}\f$ is Dawson's integral, \ref laplace::math::dawson "dawson()" on \ref dawson "its own page".

Each piece of \f$q\f$ is a polynomial of degree two in \f$u\f$. On the inner piece the exponent \f$-q\f$ is convex in \f$u\f$, so completing the square turns the inner integral into an integral of \f$e^{s^2}\f$, and that integral is Dawson's integral. On the Gaussian tail the same step gives an integral of \f$e^{-s^2}\f$, which contributes the term with \f$\sqrt{\pi V/2}\f$. Both terms of \f$Z\f$ are positive, so nothing cancels in the sum. The implementation adds the two terms in logarithms, which keeps the tail term when \f$e^{-\gamma\lambda^2/2}\f$ underflows.

## Shape of the log-density

The function \f$q\f$ and its first derivative are continuous at the knot, where \f$q'\f$ vanishes on both sides. The second derivative jumps there from \f$-1/\gamma\f$ to \f$1/V\f$, so the density is \f$C^1\f$ in \f$y\f$ at the knot. At \f$u = 0\f$ the derivative \f$q'\f$ jumps from \f$-\lambda\f$ to \f$\lambda\f$, so the log-density has a kink at the location and is \f$C^0\f$ there. The field `y_regularity` is therefore 0.

Two limits are tested. When \f$\gamma\f$ tends to zero the inner piece disappears, and the density becomes the normal density with mean \f$\mu\f$ and variance \f$V\f$ (\ref distrib_gaussian2 "gaussian2"). The test takes \f$\gamma = 10^{-14}\f$ and compares the log-density with the Gaussian one.

The second limit concerns the Laplace density \f$f_{\mathrm{L}}(y) = \tfrac{\lambda}{2} e^{-\lambda|y - \mu|}\f$ of rate \f$\lambda\f$ (\ref distrib_laplace2 "laplace2"). Let \f$x = \lambda\sqrt{\gamma/2}\f$. When \f$x\f$ is large, the tail carries no mass and the density approaches \f$f_{\mathrm{L}}\f$. The two log-densities differ exactly by
\f[
\log f(y) - \log f_{\mathrm{L}}(y) = \frac{u^2}{2\gamma} - \log\frac{\lambda Z}{2},
\f]
and \f$\log(\lambda Z/2) = 1/(2x^2) + O(x^{-4})\f$, which is the asymptotic expansion of Dawson's integral. The test checks the identity to within \f$10^{-13}\f$ for \f$0 \le u \le 0.1\f$ at \f$\lambda = 30\f$ and \f$\lambda = 300\f$, with \f$\gamma = 3\f$ and \f$V = 1\f$, and it checks the gap \f$\log(\lambda Z/2)\f$ against \f$1/(2x^2)\f$ to within \f$4/x^4\f$.

## Derivatives

The observation and the location enter through \f$u\f$ only, with \f$\partial u / \partial y = \operatorname{sign}(y - \mu)\f$ and \f$\partial u / \partial \mu = -\operatorname{sign}(y - \mu)\f$. Let \f$i\f$ be the order of differentiation in \f$\mu\f$, \f$J\f$ the order in \f$y\f$, and \f$(b, m, n)\f$ the orders in \f$(\lambda, \gamma, V)\f$. Each component of the derivative of \f$\log f\f$ is then
\f[
-(-1)^{i}\,\operatorname{sign}(y - \mu)^{\,i+J}\,\partial_u^{\,i+J}\,\partial_\lambda^{\,b}\,\partial_\gamma^{\,m}\,\partial_V^{\,n}\,q(u)
\;-\;[\,i + J = 0\,]\,\partial_\lambda^{\,b}\,\partial_\gamma^{\,m}\,\partial_V^{\,n}\,\log Z,
\f]
where \f$[\,\cdot\,]\f$ is 1 when its statement holds and 0 otherwise. The power \f$\operatorname{sign}(y - \mu)^{t}\f$ is 1 for even \f$t\f$ and \f$\operatorname{sign}(y - \mu)\f$ for odd \f$t\f$, with \f$\operatorname{sign}(0) = 0\f$.

At \f$y = \mu\f$ the odd orders in \f$\mu\f$ are zero, which is the mean of the two one-sided values. The even orders agree on the two sides and are taken from the inner piece. At a knot the value and every derivative come from the inner piece. Because \f$q\f$ is quadratic in \f$u\f$ on each piece, every component with \f$i + J \ge 3\f$ vanishes inside a piece.

The mixed partials of \f$q\f$ are closed forms. They were derived with SymPy (Meurer and others, 2017) in `tools/reference/distrib_mcp_scad_forms.py`, which compares them with SymPy's own derivatives for every order up to six at points inside each piece. The forms agree with those derivatives to \f$1.4 \times 10^{-15}\f$ in relative terms.

The derivatives of \f$\log Z\f$ are built by recursions, not written out. The constant \f$V\f$ enters \f$Z\f$ only through the factor \f$\sqrt{\pi V/2}\f$, so
\f[
Z = 2\left[A(\lambda, \gamma) + B(\lambda, \gamma)\sqrt{\pi V/2}\right],
\f]
and the table of \f$Z\f$ over the three parameters comes from two tables over \f$(\lambda, \gamma)\f$ and from the derivatives of \f$V^{1/2}\f$. The tables over \f$(\lambda, \gamma)\f$ come from the exponential and from Dawson's integral by the chain rule. The table of \f$\log Z\f$ then comes from the logarithm of the table of \f$Z\f$. Three recursions do this work. Each binomial coefficient \f$\binom{\alpha'}{\beta}\f$ below is a product over the variables. In each recursion \f$\alpha\f$ is a multi-index, \f$r\f$ is the first index with \f$\alpha_r > 0\f$, and \f$\alpha' = \alpha - e_r\f$ with \f$e_r\f$ the unit multi-index in direction \f$r\f$. The letter \f$\gamma\f$ is the concavity throughout this page and never a multi-index.

The table of \f$e^{\varphi}\f$ comes from the table of \f$\varphi\f$:
\f[
\partial^{\alpha} e^{\varphi} = \sum_{\beta \le \alpha'} \binom{\alpha'}{\beta}\, \partial^{\beta} e^{\varphi}\; \partial^{\alpha' - \beta + e_r} \varphi.
\f]

The table of \f$f^{(k)}(x)\f$, the \f$k\f$-th derivative of a function \f$f\f$ at a point \f$x\f$ that is a function of the parameters, is built downward from \f$k\f$ equal to the top order:
\f[
\partial^{\alpha} f^{(k)}(x) = \sum_{\beta \le \alpha'} \binom{\alpha'}{\beta}\, \partial^{\beta} f^{(k+1)}(x)\; \partial^{\alpha' - \beta + e_r} x.
\f]

The table of \f$\log Z\f$ comes from the table of \f$Z\f$, with \f$Z_{\mathbf{0}}\f$ the value of the table at the point:
\f[
\partial^{\alpha} \log Z = \frac{1}{Z_{\mathbf{0}}}\Big(\partial^{\alpha} Z - \sum_{\mathbf{0} \ne \beta \le \alpha'} \binom{\alpha'}{\beta}\, \partial^{\beta} Z\; \partial^{\alpha' - \beta + e_r} \log Z\Big).
\f]

The first two recursions act on tables over \f$(\lambda, \gamma)\f$, and the last on the table over \f$(\lambda, \gamma, V)\f$. A table over three variables is packed over the multi-indices of total order at most the order of the derivative, which at order six is 84 entries against 343 for a full cube.

## Cumulative functions

Let \f$U(u) = \int_u^\infty e^{-q(t)}\,\mathrm{d}t\f$ for \f$u \ge 0\f$, the integral of the unnormalized density beyond the distance \f$u\f$. It has a closed form, with \f$\Phi\f$ the standard normal cumulative distribution function:
\f[
U(u) = \begin{cases} \sqrt{2\gamma}\; e^{-q(u)}\,\mathcal{D}\!\left(\dfrac{\gamma\lambda - u}{\sqrt{2\gamma}}\right) + e^{-\gamma\lambda^2/2}\sqrt{\pi V/2}, & u \le \gamma\lambda, \\[8pt] 2\, e^{-\gamma\lambda^2/2}\sqrt{\pi V/2}\; \Phi\!\left(-\dfrac{u - \gamma\lambda}{\sqrt{V}}\right), & u > \gamma\lambda. \end{cases}
\f]
The cumulative distribution function is \f$F(y) = U(|y - \mu|)/Z\f$ for \f$y \le \mu\f$ and \f$F(y) = 1 - U(|y - \mu|)/Z\f$ for \f$y > \mu\f$. Every term of \f$U\f$ is a product of positive factors, so no difference is taken between two nearly equal numbers.

The functions `log_cdf()` and `log_ccdf()` compute the logarithm of the side whose probability is small, which can fall below the smallest positive double. They compute the other side as one minus a plain ratio. A logarithm of the small side would carry the rounding error of its own exponent into the result, one ulp per unit of the size of \f$\log F\f$.

The derivatives of \f$\log F\f$ in the parameters, the expected information and the quantile are not members of this law: the \ref distrib_generic "generic routes" compute them.

## Non-smooth points

`y_kinks()` declares three points, \f$\mu - \gamma\lambda\f$, \f$\mu\f$ and \f$\mu + \gamma\lambda\f$, each with `continuous_order` 0. The two knots are not kinks, since the second derivative of the density jumps there and the first does not. They are declared so that a quadrature which takes the declared points as cuts has a panel end at every knot.

`param_kinks(j, y)` gives, for each parameter, the points of its own axis where the log-density is not smooth. For \f$\mu\f$ the point is \f$\mu = y\f$, with order 0. For \f$\lambda\f$, when \f$u = |y - \mu| > 0\f$, the point is \f$\lambda = u/\gamma\f$, the value at which the knot reaches \f$u\f$, with order 1. For \f$\gamma\f$, when \f$u > 0\f$, the point is \f$\gamma = u/\lambda\f$, also with order 1. The tail variance has no points.

## Accuracy and limits

The table lists the worst errors measured against mpmath references (mpmath), computed at 300 and 400 bits, with the bounds of the tests. The same test loop covers the properized MCP and SCAD laws, so each figure is the worst over both laws. The measurements were made on gcc (Kokkos Serial and OpenMP) and on CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER), and the host against CUDA row compares the two backends. The method of measurement is described on \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured".

| Quantity | Worst measured | Test bound |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Log-density, order 0 | \f$3.66\f$ ulp | \f$6\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 1 | \f$11.4\f$ ulp | \f$18\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 2 | \f$11.5\f$ ulp | \f$18\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 3 | \f$8.75\f$ ulp | \f$14\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 4 | \f$11.2\f$ ulp | \f$18\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 5 | \f$41.7\f$ ulp | \f$64\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 6 | \f$162\f$ ulp | \f$250\f$ ulp |
| \f$\log F\f$ and \f$\log(1 - F)\f$ | \f$2.46\f$ ulp of \f$\max(\lvert \log F \rvert, 1)\f$ | \f$4\f$ ulp |
| Normalizing constant \f$Z\f$ | \f$1.33\f$ ulp | \f$2\f$ ulp |
| \f$F\f$ and \f$1 - F\f$ against the quadrature of the definition (bound on \f$F\f$) | \f$6.8 \times 10^{-16}\f$ relative | \f$10^{-11}\f$ relative |
| Mass of the density, every knot declared as a cut | \f$4.4 \times 10^{-16}\f$ | \f$10^{-15}\f$ |
| Log-density against the normal limit | \f$8.0 \times 10^{-14}\f$ absolute | \f$10^{-10}\f$ |
| Cumulative function of the quantile against \f$p\f$, for \f$p\f$ from \f$10^{-8}\f$ to \f$1 - 10^{-8}\f$ | \f$2.5 \times 10^{-15}\f$ relative | \f$10^{-10}\f$ relative |
| Host against CUDA, sum of the components of each order | \f$1.18 \times 10^{-15}\f$ at order 1, \f$2.1 \times 10^{-16}\f$ to \f$5.5 \times 10^{-16}\f$ at the others | \f$2 \times 10^{-15}\f$ |

The log-density bounds are in ulp of the largest component of each order at the point, as the test defines them. The figure \f$2.46\f$ ulp refers to \f$\max(\lvert \log F \rvert, 1)\f$. In ulp of \f$\log F\f$ itself the same rows reach \f$437\f$ ulp, which is the conditioning of a Gaussian tail: the argument of \f$\Phi\f$ carries one ulp, and the logarithm of \f$\Phi\f$ has slope \f$-z\f$ in its argument, \f$z\f$ the standardized distance beyond the knot. The expected information of this law is not closed; the test checks that the generic route yields finite components.

The references and the derivative checks are described in `docs/numerics.md`.

## Kokkos usage

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/mcp.hpp>

void eval_mcp(Kokkos::View<const double*> y, Kokkos::View<double*> out,
              double mu, double lambda, double gamma, double tail_sigma2) {
  const laplace::distrib::mcp_distrib d{mu, lambda, gamma, tail_sigma2};
  Kokkos::parallel_for("mcp_eval", y.extent(0), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    out(i) = d.logpdf(y(i)) + d.logpdf_d1(y(i))[0] + d.cdf(y(i));
  });
}
```

## Members

Struct \ref laplace::distrib::mcp_distrib "mcp_distrib" has the structure of \ref distrib_core "a univariate distribution" with `n_params = 4`, with the fields `mu`, `lambda`, `gamma` and `tail_sigma2`. Its members are `kind`, `n_params`, `y_regularity`, `params`, `logpdf()`, `logpdf_d1()` to `logpdf_d6()`, `logpdf_dk()`, `logpdf_dy_dk()`, `cdf()`, `ccdf()`, `log_cdf()`, `log_ccdf()`, `support_lower()`, `support_upper()`, `center()`, `scale()`, `param_kinks()`, `y_kinks()` and `start_value()`. The member `scale()` returns \f$1/\lambda + \sqrt{V}\f$, a rough spread of the law. The member `start_value()` takes the sample median as \f$\mu\f$, the reciprocal of the mean absolute deviation about the median as \f$\lambda\f$, the value 3 as \f$\gamma\f$ and the sample variance as \f$V\f$ (\ref distrib_start "Starting values").

## References

- C.-H. Zhang (2010), Nearly unbiased variable selection under minimax concave penalty, The Annals of Statistics, 38(2), 894-942. https://doi.org/10.1214/09-aos729
- J. Fan and R. Li (2001), Variable Selection via Nonconcave Penalized Likelihood and its Oracle Properties, Journal of the American Statistical Association, 96(456), 1348-1360. https://doi.org/10.1198/016214501753382273
- A. Meurer and others (2017), SymPy: symbolic computing in Python, PeerJ Computer Science, 3, e103. https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.103
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
*/
